  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are partly sunny at this time\nacross the area. Enough instability and moisture have allowed\nisolated to scattered showers and one or two rumbles of thunder\nacross portions of Elko and northern White Pine counties this\nafternoon. Look for the shower activity to increase later on\ntonight. Overnight lows will be in the 50s.\n\nThe ramp up of shower activity is due to the remnants of \nhurricane Rachel joining forces with two upper level troughs.\nThese systems will eventually push subtropical moisture northward\nacross southern California and through southern and central\nNevada. Periods of rain showers will increase in areal coverage \nand intensity as the upper low consolidates over the states of\nOregon and Washington. Total QPF amounts for the first twenty-four\nhours looks impressive with amounts greater than an inch. With\nrespect to the probability, there are sixty to eighty percent\nprobability of greater than an inch across much of central and\nnortheast Nevada. As a result, have expanded the flash flood watch\nto include southern Lander/Eureka counties and Elko county\nbeginning tonight at 11pm and lasting until early Monday morning.\nThe air mass will be very moist with forecast precipitable water\nvalues climbing into the seven to eight tenths, which would make\nthis near a daily record amount for October 11. Highs will only be\nin the 60s and winds will continue to remain gusty and strong. One\nconcern tomorrow will be the isolated thunderstorm probability,\nwhich sits at about twenty percent over the forecast area\ncontained in the flash flood watch. There is slightly more CAPE\nand instability for tomorrow where cells could produce very heavy\nrainfall. Main concerns are the burn scar locations highlighted in\nthe flood watch and these areas will need to be watched. \n\nLighter rain showers are expected to continue over most of \nnorthern and central Nevada Monday night into Tuesday morning with\nshowers ending from west to east Tuesday afternoon and overnight.\nDry but noticeably cooler conditions are expected Wednesday \nthrough at least next Friday. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Adjustments were made to tweak\nthe NBM forecast with regards to PoP, weather, wind speeds, and\nwind gusts. Confidence remains high in the forecast with respect \nto the strong wind gusts in Central Nevada this evening, with \nmoderate to high confidence in strong winds Sunday. High forecast \nconfidence regarding significant rainfall in central and eastern \nNevada beginning late tonight through Monday. High forecast \nconfidence in a cooling trend through Monday.",
